@math{X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_n}
@end iftex 
be independent and identically distributed 
@html
\({\it Normal}(0, 1)\)
@end html
@ifinfo
@math{Normal(0, 1)}
@end ifinfo
@iftex
@math{{\it Normal}(0, 1)}
@end iftex 
variables.  Then
@html
$$
X^2 = \sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2
$$
@end html
@ifinfo
@math{X^2 = sum(X_i^2, i, 1, n)}
@end ifinfo
@tex
$$X^2 = \sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2$$
@end tex


is said to follow a chi-square distribution with @math{n} degrees of
freedom.

@anchor{pdf_chi2}
@deffn {Function} pdf_chi2 (@var{x},@var{n})
Returns the value at @var{x} of the density function of a Chi-square
random variable 
@html
\(\chi^2(n)\)
@end html
@ifinfo
@math{Chi^2()}
@end ifinfo
@iftex
@math{\chi^2(n)}
@end iftex
, with @math{n>0}.
The 
@html
\(\chi^2(n)\)
@end html
@ifinfo
@math{Chi^2()}
@end ifinfo
@iftex
@math{\chi^2(n)}
@end iftex 
random variable is equivalent to the 
@html
\(\Gamma\left(n/2,2\right)\)
@end html
@ifinfo
@math{Gamma(n/2,2)}
@end ifinfo
@iftex
@math{\Gamma\left(n/2,2\right)}
@end iftex
.

The pdf is

@html
$$
f(x; n) =
\cases{
 \displaystyle{x^{n/2-1} e^{-x/2} \over 2^{n/2}
 \Gamma\left(\displaystyle{n\over 2}\right)} & for $x
 > 0$ \cr
\cr
 0 & otherwise
}
$$
@end html
@ifinfo

@example
             [  n/2 - 1   - x/2
             [ x        %e      unit_step(x)
             [ -----------------------------     for x >= 0
             [               n   n/2
   f(x, n) = [         gamma(-) 2
             [               2
             [
             [ 0                                 otherwise
@end example

@end ifinfo
@tex
$$f(x; n) =
\cases{
 \displaystyle{x^{n/2-1} e^{-x/2} \over 2^{n/2}
 \Gamma\left(\displaystyle{n\over 2}\right)} & for $x
 > 0$ \cr
\cr
 0 & otherwise
}$$
@end tex


